# zlib 1.3.2 — our delta from pristine upstream.
#
# Upstream: https://github.com/madler/zlib/releases/download/v1.3.2/zlib-1.3.2.tar.gz
#           sha256 bb329a0a2cd0274d05519d61c667c062e06990d72e125ee2dfa8de64f0119d16
#           GPG: zlib-1.3.2.tar.gz.asc verifies as a Good signature from
#           "Mark Adler <madler@alumni.caltech.edu>", DSA key
#           5ED46A6721D365587791E2AA783FCD8E58BCAFBA — upstream's own author.
#           (No independent trust path to that key; corroboration, not a root of trust.)
# Apply from sdk/lib/3rdparty/zlib/ with: patch -p1 < vendor-patches/zlib.patch
# zlib.h and zconf.h live in sdk/include/reactos/libs/zlib/ and are PRISTINE, so they
# are not in this patch.
#
# SCOPE: zlib PROPER IS PRISTINE — every file of the compression library (adler32,
# crc32, deflate, inflate, inftrees, trees, zutil, gz*, compress, uncompr, infback and
# both public headers) is byte-identical to upstream 1.3.2. Nothing here touches bounds
# checking, allocation, or the inflate/deflate state machines. The whole delta is TEN
# LINES across three files in contrib/minizip, the ZIP-archive helper.
#
# WHAT IT DOES: minizip has two file backends — a stdio FILE* one (ioapi.c) and a
# win32-HANDLE one (iowin32.c). We compile out the stdio half and the zipOpen2/unzOpen2
# entry points that depend on it.
#
# WHY, precisely: it is NOT merely that we prefer the win32 backend. ioapi.c's
# fopen64_file_func calls FOPEN_FUNC, which resolves to fopen64 — and fopen64,
# ftello64 and fseeko64 do not exist in our CRT. Without the guard the build fails with
# "call to undeclared function 'fopen64'". Confirmed by removing the guard and
# rebuilding during the 1.3.2 bump, rather than inherited from the old note.
#
# WHY NOT A BUILD FLAG: __REACTOS__ already IS one; what cannot move into the build is
# WHICH REGIONS it guards, because upstream has no such conditionals to switch on.
# Deleting the stdio backend outright would be a larger delta, not a smaller one.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1.2.13 -> 1.3.2 (2026-09-20): THE DELTA SHRANK ON ITS OWN
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The patch went from 14 lines to 10, and the CMakeLists lost both of its warning
# suppressions. Nothing was given up to get that; upstream fixed all three:
#
#   * THE NOUNCRYPT HUNK IS GONE. 1.2.13's unzip.c force-defined NOUNCRYPT
#     (`#ifndef NOUNCRYPT / #define NOUNCRYPT / #endif`), which DISABLES traditional
#     PKWARE decryption, and our patch wrapped that in __REACTOS__ to get decryption
#     back. 1.3.x removed the force-define, so decryption is on by default for
#     everyone and the hunk has nothing left to do. Behaviour is unchanged: NOUNCRYPT
#     is still undefined in our build, so crypt.h is still included.
#   * -Wno-deprecated-non-prototype is gone. It existed for 1.2.13's K&R-style
#     definitions; 1.3.0 converted the whole library to prototypes.
#   * -Wno-unused-variable on minizip is gone. It existed for four `mode_fopen` locals
#     in iowin32.c that only the stdio backend used; 1.3.x no longer leaves them unused.
#
# THREE NEW UPSTREAM FILES had to be vendored for minizip to build:
#   ints.h            fixed-width integer typedefs derived from <limits.h>
#   skipset.h         a skiplist set, used by zip.c for duplicate-name detection
#   LICENSE.Info-Zip  see below — this one is a licence fact, not a build input
#
# LICENCE: minizip IS NOT UNDER THE ZLIB LICENCE. 1.3.x began shipping an explicit
# contrib/minizip/LICENSE.Info-Zip (Info-ZIP licence, 2009-Jan-02 version), and
# unzip.c/crypt.h carry "Copyright (c) 1990-2000 Info-ZIP" alongside Gilles Vollant's
# minizip copyright. This was always true of the code — 1.2.13's unzip.c says so too —
# but upstream had never stated it as a licence file, and our registry recorded only
# `Zlib`. Now recorded in vendor.yaml under license_extra. We build minizip into rapps
# and zipfldr, so the obligation is live, not theoretical.
#
